"[2511.01866v1] EdgeReasoning: Characterizing Reasoning LLM Deployment on Edge GPUs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01866v1)\nciteturn2281view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01866v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Oct 2025]\nL9: # Title:EdgeReasoning: Characterizing Reasoning LLM Deployment on Edge GPUs\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Benjamin Kubwimana , cite6†Qijing Huang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled EdgeReasoning: Characterizing Reasoning LLM Deployment on Edge GPUs, by Benjamin Kubwimana and 1 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite7†View PDF cite8†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Edge intelligence paradigm is increasingly demanded by the emerging autonomous systems, such as robotics. Beyond ensuring privacy-preserving operation and resilience in connectivity-limited environments, edge deployment offers significant energy and cost advantages over cloud-based solutions. However, deploying large language models (LLMs) for reasoning tasks on edge GPUs faces critical challenges from strict latency constraints and limited computational resources.\nL17: To navigate these constraints, developers must balance multiple design factors - choosing reasoning versus non-reasoning architectures, selecting appropriate model sizes, allocating token budgets, and applying test-time scaling strategies - to meet target latency and optimize accuracy. Yet guidance on optimal combinations of these variables remains scarce. In this work, we present EdgeReasoning, a comprehensive study characterizing the deployment of reasoning LLMs on edge GPUs.\nL18: We systematically quantify latency-accuracy tradeoffs across various LLM architectures and model sizes. We systematically evaluate prompt-based and model-tuning-based techniques for reducing reasoning token length while maintaining performance quality. We further profile test-time scaling methods with varying degrees of parallelism to maximize accuracy under strict latency budgets.\nL19: Through these analyses, EdgeReasoning maps the Pareto frontier of achievable accuracy-latency configurations, offering systematic guidance for optimal edge deployment of reasoning LLMs.\nL20: Comments:  | Published in the Proceedings of the 2025 IEEE International Symposium on Workload Characterization (IISWC 2025)\nL21: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite9†arXiv:2511.01866 [cs.DC]\nL23:    | (or cite10†arXiv:2511.01866v1 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL24:    | cite11†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.01866†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Benjamin Kubwimana [cite12†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Tue, 21 Oct 2025 04:18:25 UTC (389 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled EdgeReasoning: Characterizing Reasoning LLM Deployment on Edge GPUs, by Benjamin Kubwimana and 1 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite7†View PDF L37:   * cite8†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite13†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite14†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.DC\nL44: \nL45: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L46: \nL47: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2025-11 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite20†cs L52: cite21†cs.AI L53: cite22†cs.AR L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite23†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite24†Google Scholar†scholar.goo